/** @page jniinitguide JVM Initialization

    @tableofcontents

    The JVM is initialized when the module is loaded.  It is possible to disable JIT when the module is loaded,
    however the module is loaded an initialized before module parse commands are processed, therefore to tell the
    module to disable JIT, the following environment variable must be set to \c 1:
    - <tt>QORE_JNI_DISABLE_JIT=1</tt>

    Heap sizes can be controlled by the following environment variables:
    - \c QORE_JNI_MIN_HEAP_SIZE: will set the <tt><b>-Xms</b></tt> JVM initialization option for the minimum heap size
    - \c QORE_JNI_MAX_HEAP_SIZE: will set the <tt><b>-Xmx</b></tt> JVM initialization option for the maximum heap size
    - \c QORE_JNI_DISABLE_NATIVE_ACCESS: when set to \c 1, do not add
      <tt><b>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</b></tt> to JVM initialization options

    For example:
    @verbatim
# to initialize the JVM with a minimum and maximum heap size of 20MB
QORE_JNI_MIN_HEAP_SIZE=20m QORE_JNI_MAX_HEAP_SIZE=20m qore -l jni script.q
    @endverbatim

    @section jni_signals Signals Used By the JVM

    The JVM requires signals to run properly, and, while %Qore initializes the JVM with reduced signals (using the
    <tt>-Xrs</tt> argument to the JVM), certain signals are critical to the operation of the JVM and cannot be used by
    %Qore programs.

    The signals in the following table are reserved by the JVM.

    <b>Signals Reserved by the JVM</b>
    |!Signal|!Description
    |\c SIGTRAP|Trap exception
    |\c SIGSEGV|Segmentation violation
    |\c SIGBUS|Bus error
    |\c SIGCHLD|Child process terminated
    |\c SIGILL|Illegal instruction
    |\c SIGFPE|Floating-point exception

    If any %Qore code has a signal handler assigned to any of the above signals (for the signals that can be
    assigned), the \c jni module cannot be initialized and will fail to load.

    When the \c jni module creates the JVM, it always initializes it with reduced signals, also when \c libjsig (the
    JVM's signal-chaining library) is preloaded or linked into the process: %Qore is the host process and owns the
    asynchronous signals, such as \c SIGTERM, \c SIGINT, \c SIGHUP and \c SIGQUIT, which %Qore programs can handle
    with @ref Qore::set_signal_handler() "set_signal_handler()"; without a handler, \c SIGTERM, \c SIGINT and
    \c SIGHUP end the process through %Qore.  (When a Java process loads %Qore and so has created the JVM itself, the
    Java host decides the JVM's options.)  With reduced signals, the JVM:
    - still handles the synchronous signals that it needs to run (\c SIGSEGV, \c SIGBUS, \c SIGFPE and \c SIGILL),
      which it uses for implicit null checks, stack overflow checks and safepoints; \c libjsig, if present, chains
      them with the handlers of other runtimes in the process; it also keeps its handlers for \c SIGUSR2 (which it
      uses internally), \c SIGPIPE and \c SIGXFSZ
    - does not run Java shutdown hooks when the process gets a shutdown signal; they run when the JVM is destroyed as
      the process ends normally, and do not run when %Qore ends the process immediately, as for a shutdown signal
      without a handler
    - does not print a thread dump for \c SIGQUIT; use <tt>jcmd <i>pid</i> Thread.print</tt> or \c jstack instead
      (the JVM starts its attach listener at startup when it runs with reduced signals, so these tools do not need a
      signal); the listener's socket (<tt>/tmp/.java_pid<i>pid</i></tt>) is removed when the process ends with
      \c exit(), and remains when %Qore ends the process immediately
    - refuses Java handlers for \c SIGTERM, \c SIGINT and \c SIGHUP (as it always does for \c SIGQUIT) registered
      with the internal Java signal API (\c jdk.internal.misc.Signal or \c sun.misc.Signal); Java code can still
      replace the JVM's handlers for other signals, such as \c SIGUSR2, with this API

    The JVM reads \c JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS first and the module adds <tt>-Xrs</tt> after the options in
    \c QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS, so <tt>-XX:-ReduceSignalUsage</tt> there has no effect; it must not be set in
    \c _JAVA_OPTIONS either, which the JVM applies last: a shutdown signal would then end the process with Java's
    \c System.exit() (see @ref jni_process_exit), and %Qore signal handlers would not be called.

    @section jni_process_exit Process Exit From Java

    When Java code calls \c System.exit() or \c Runtime.halt() in a JVM that the \c jni module created, the process
    ends through %Qore's process exit, as it does with the %Qore @ref Qore::exit() "exit()" function, with the status
    given (qore#6698):
    - for \c System.exit(), Java runs its shutdown hooks first, as usual; \c Runtime.halt() runs none, as usual; both
      also end the process when they are called in a Java thread that %Qore did not create
    - the JVM then stops all Java threads and calls the module's exit hook on its VM Thread instead of C \c exit();
      the hook calls \c qore_exit_process(), which the %Qore \c exit() function calls too
    - as the VM Thread is not a %Qore thread, %Qore ends the process immediately (with \c _Exit()), as \c exit()
      does when it is called in a process with other %Qore threads: no %Qore code runs after Java's shutdown hooks
      (neither the hooks set with @ref Qore::register_exit_hook() "register_exit_hook()", which %Qore runs only when
      a program ends normally, nor the delete functions of modules), the JVM is not destroyed (its threads stay
      stopped), and the thread whose Java call made the call never returns from it; the attach listener's socket
      (<tt>/tmp/.java_pid<i>pid</i></tt>) is removed on Linux

    Before, the JVM called C \c exit() on its VM Thread, which ran libqore's static destructors while %Qore threads
    were still running, and the process aborted (<tt>external native threads still active while stopping the cleanup
    worker</tt>) when a \c ThreadPool worker was active.

    When a Java process loads %Qore and so has created the JVM itself, the Java host decides how the JVM ends the
    process.
*/
